<html>
<head>
    <%- include("partials/header1.ejs") %>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/public/css/styles.css">

    <style>

        /* Info card styling */
        .info-card {
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
            padding: 25px;
            border-radius: 14px;
            border: 1px solid #E2E8F0;
            backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
            box-shadow: 0 8px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.12);
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }

        h2 {
            margin-bottom: 12px;
        }
    </style>
</head>

<body>

    <!-- Shared Navbar -->
    <%- include("partials/navbar.ejs") %>
     <!-- ⭐ UNIVERSAL Background -->
    <%- include('partials/globalBackground') %>

    <div class="temp-visa-bg">

        <div class="content-box">

            <h2>Temporary Class Visas</h2>
            <p>Explore New Zealand’s temporary visa categories, requirements, and available pathways.Find out what is new with Immigration New Zealand <a href="https://www.immigration.govt.nz/about-us/news-centre/"><strong>here</strong></a>. </p>

            <div class="info-card">
                <a href="https://www.immigration.govt.nz/visas/visitor-visa/"><h3>Visitor Visa</h3></a>
                <p>The Visitor Visa is designed for people travelling to New Zealand for
                    <strong>short-term purposes</strong>, such as tourism, visiting family or friends,
                    or attending short business activities. Most Visitor Visas allow stays of
                    <strong>up to 9 months within an 18-month period</strong>, although this can vary
                    depending on nationality, travel history, and purpose of travel.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                    Applicants must demonstrate <strong>genuine visitor intent</strong>, meaning they
                    plan to leave New Zealand before their visa expires. Visitors are generally
                    <strong>not permitted to work</strong> and may only undertake
                    <strong>short courses or training of up to three months</strong>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                    In certain cases, visitors already in New Zealand may be able to apply for a
                    <strong>visa extension</strong>, provided they continue to meet all conditions.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                    The Visitor Visa is often a first step for people exploring New Zealand, but it
                    does <strong>not automatically lead to residence</strong>.
                    </p>

            </div>

            <div class="info-card">
                <a href="https://www.immigration.govt.nz/study/study-visas/visas-for-studying-in-new-zealand/"><h3>Student Visas</h3></a>
                <p>A Student Visa allows international students to study
                <strong>full-time</strong> at an approved New Zealand education provider.
                Applicants must hold an <strong>offer of place</strong> from a recognised
                institution.
                </p>

                <p>
                Student Visas often include <strong>work rights</strong>, depending on the level
                and duration of study. Many students can work
                <strong>up to 20 hours per week during term time</strong> and full-time during
                scheduled holidays.
                </p>

                <p>
                Applicants must show they have <strong>sufficient funds</strong> to cover tuition
                fees, living expenses, and onward travel. Health insurance is
                <strong>mandatory</strong> for the duration of study.
                </p>

                <p>
                While a Student Visa does not guarantee residence, it may support
                <strong>post-study work opportunities</strong> that can lead to skilled work or
                residence pathways.
                </p>

            </div>

            <div class="info-card">
                <a href="https://www.immigration.govt.nz/visas/accredited-employer-work-visa/"><h3>Accredited Employer Work Visa</h3></a>
                <p>The Accredited Employer Work Visa (AEWV) allows migrants to work for a
                    <strong>New Zealand employer who is accredited</strong> to hire overseas workers.
                    The visa is tied to a specific job offer.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                    The role must meet defined <strong>wage, skill, and occupation requirements</strong>.
                    In most cases, the offered salary must meet or exceed the
                    <strong>New Zealand median wage</strong>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                    AEWVs are usually granted for <strong>up to three years</strong>, depending on the
                    role and pay level. Visa holders are typically restricted to working for the
                    approved employer.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                    This visa may support <strong>future residence eligibility</strong>, particularly
                    for roles on the Green List or in long-term skill shortage areas.
                    </p>

            </div>

            <div class="info-card">
                <a href="https://www.immigration.govt.nz/work/working-holiday-visas/"><h3>Working Holiday Visas</h3></a>
                <p>Working Holiday Visas allow young people from
                <strong>eligible countries</strong> to travel and work in New Zealand for a
                limited period, usually <strong>12 months</strong>.
                </p>

                <p>
                Applicants must meet <strong>age requirements</strong>, hold sufficient funds,
                and have medical insurance. Work is permitted but intended to be
                <strong>temporary or seasonal</strong>.
                </p>

                <p>
                Visa holders may study or train for <strong>up to six months</strong> in total.
                Some visas restrict how long a person can work for one employer.
                </p>

                <p>
                While not a direct residence pathway, Working Holiday Visas can provide
                <strong>valuable New Zealand work experience</strong> and support future visa
                transitions.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>

</body>
</html>
